        "fullLyrics": "[00:12.04] You get a shiver in the dark, it's raining in the park, but meantime\n[00:19.65] South of the river, you stop and you hold everything\n[00:25.94] A band is blowing Dixie, double-four time\n[00:32.65] You feel alright when you hear that music ring\n[00:37.62] \n[00:41.79] Well, now you step inside, but you don't see too many faces\n[00:48.74] Coming in out of the rain to hear the jazz go down\n[00:55.51] Competition in other places\n[01:01.58] Ah, but the horns, they blowing that sound\n[01:06.16] \n[01:09.02] Way on down south\n[01:12.13] Way on down south, London town\n[01:16.69] \n[01:26.74] You check out Guitar George, he knows all the chords\n[01:33.96] Mind, he's strictly rhythm, he doesn't want to make it cry or sing\n[01:40.68] Yes and an old guitar is all he can afford\n[01:47.09] When he gets up under the lights to play his thing\n[01:51.81] \n[01:55.89] And Harry doesn't mind if he doesn't make the scene\n[02:03.13] He's got a daytime job, he's doing alright\n[02:09.12] He can play the honky-tonk like anything\n[02:15.78] Saving it up for Friday night\n[02:20.31] \n[02:23.58] With the Sultans\n[02:26.31] With the Sultans of Swing\n[02:30.28] \n[02:42.00] And a crowd of young boys, they're fooling around in the corner\n[02:48.56] Drunk and dressed in their best brown baggies and their platform soles\n[02:54.73] They don't give a damn about any trumpet-playing band\n[03:01.96] It ain't what they call rock and roll\n[03:06.55] \n[03:08.82] And the Sultans\n[03:11.85] Yeah, the Sultans, they played creole\n[03:16.96] Creole\n[03:20.31] \n[04:12.78] And then the man, he steps right up to the microphone\n[04:17.27] \n[04:20.28] And says at last, just as the time bell rings\n[04:26.66] \"Goodnight, now it's time to go home\"\n[04:32.98] Then he makes it fast with one more thing\n[04:39.07] \"We are the Sultans\n[04:42.22] We are the Sultans of Swing\"\n[04:44.41]",
